          "The passage of time has a peculiar way of reshaping the contours of one’s existence, often in ways that are neither dramatic nor immediately perceptible. For the author, this transformation unfolded incrementally, marked by shifts in routine, perception, and the ways in which they interacted with their own mind. The catalyst for this reflection was not a singular event but a compounding of years spent navigating a life that increasingly prioritized external stimuli over internal reflection. What began as a subtle adjustment—filling the gaps between tasks with auditory or digital input—gradually evolved into a near-instinctive behavior. This was not a conscious decision but a response to the encroachment of obligations, which eroded what had once been moments of unstructured stillness. Now, even the most mundane activities, such as lifting weights or preparing a meal, were accompanied by the hum of a podcast, the narration of an audiobook, or thescrolling of a social feed. The habit was so entrenched that the absence of such stimulation felt almost unnatural, as though the mind had become conditioned to expect a constant stream of input to sustain its motion. This dependency on external voices, however, came at a cost: the quiet moments that once allowed for unfiltered thought became something to be replaced rather than embraced.",
